WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.180
When does an apparently obvious

2
00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:05.120
NP certificate fail? The photographed

3
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.620
subset questions distinguish one solution,

4
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.580
two solutions, a binary-specified number

5
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.020
of solutions, and a counting

6
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.340
output. We will inspect certificate

7
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.080
length as carefully as verification

8
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.840
time, without turning a failed

9
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.900
certificate idea into an unsupported

10
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.160
impossibility claim.

11
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.060
For existence of one subset

12
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.860
summing to a target, use

13
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.440
one selection bit per input

14
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.720
index. Add the selected binary

15
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.440
integers and compare the result

16
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.020
to the target. The bit

17
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.560
mask has linear length in

18
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.940
the number of entries, and

19
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.360
intermediate sums have only polynomially

20
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:38.940
many bits.

21
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.020
For existence of two distinct

22
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.080
subsets with that sum, give

23
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.560
two masks. Verify both sums

24
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.940
and check the masks differ

25
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.340
in at least one index

26
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.500
bit. On the repeated-value example,

27
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.320
choosing the first one or

28
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.740
the second one produces different

29
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.300
subsets even though the selected

30
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.820
numeric values look identical.

31
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.460
For a fixed constant number

32
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.440
of requested solutions, a fixed

33
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.080
number of masks is still

34
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.660
polynomial-sized. But the photo also

35
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.680
allows k itself in binary.

36
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.960
Then k can be exponentially

37
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.520
large in its written length,

38
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.460
so listing k masks may

39
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.480
take superpolynomial space in the

40
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:15.920
full input.

41
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.100
This exposes a gap in

42
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.640
that proposed NP proof: checking

43
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.460
a gigantic supplied list quickly

44
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.900
relative to the list length

45
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.820
is not enough. The witness

46
00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.640
length must be polynomial in

47
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.840
the original instance length. Its

48
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.380
failure does not prove that

49
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.320
every other possible certificate scheme

50
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.020
fails or that the language

51
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.140
is outside NP.

52
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.580
The question asking how many

53
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.100
subsets sum to the target

54
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.820
asks for a numeric output.

55
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.020
NP, as defined in this

56
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.280
course, is a class of

57
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.720
decision languages with yes-or-no answers.

58
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.540
A related threshold decision problem

59
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.340
is meaningful, but it is

60
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:54.440
a new formal problem requiring

61
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.240
its own classification argument.

62
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.780
The zero-valued entries in the

63
00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.760
photo’s input make index discipline

64
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.620
especially useful. Including or omitting

65
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.340
a zero changes the subset

66
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.860
mask even though it preserves

67
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.560
the sum. Deleting zero entries

68
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.080
can preserve existence of some

69
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.980
solution while changing the number

70
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.480
of distinct solutions, so transformations

71
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.440
must match their exact decision

72
00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:17.900
question.

73
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.140
The usual dynamic program for

74
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.760
Subset Sum has a table

75
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.500
indexed by the target value.

76
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.380
That value can be huge

77
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.060
compared with its binary encoding,

78
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.020
even though a chosen subset

79
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.880
is easy to verify. Polynomial

80
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.440
certificate verification and a deterministic

81
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.960
polynomial algorithm are separate claims,

82
00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.240
so the table-size argument must

83
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.920
use the original input length.

84
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.280
A sound verifier must also

85
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.680
reject malformed masks and incorrect

86
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.880
sums, not merely accept the

87
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.380
example it was designed around.

88
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.540
For two-solution certificates, duplicated copies

89
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.800
of the same mask must

90
00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.960
not satisfy the distinctness requirement.

91
00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.940
State all these checks before

92
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.540
declaring the problem in NP.

93
00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.280
Things to take note of.

94
00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.880
Keep subset indices distinct from

95
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.160
repeated numeric values, account for

96
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.060
binary k when measuring witness

97
00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.440
size, and distinguish counting functions

98
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.380
from decision languages. If your

99
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.100
proposed certificate is too long,

100
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.360
identify that proof gap honestly

101
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.040
rather than claiming an unproved

102
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:19.620
separation.
